WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.940
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.287
Building the Attribute-Driven Migration.

3
00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:08.619
This is Chapter 8: Ansible beside Arc.

4
00:00:08.619 --> 00:00:13.517
Microsoft's role connects a server to
Azure Arc, but it doesn't know which tool

5
00:00:13.517 --> 00:00:18.337
owns the server's settings, which tool
patches it, or which wave it belongs to.

6
00:00:18.337 --> 00:00:20.532
The configuration database knows,

7
00:00:20.532 --> 00:00:23.787
and the inventory has to
carry that to every connect.

8
00:00:23.787 --> 00:00:28.453
We'll start from Microsoft's own role,
see what it leaves out, then walk the

9
00:00:28.453 --> 00:00:33.276
inventory, the wave plan, the onboarding
playbook, two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:33.276 --> 00:00:38.800
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.062
Why does this chapter matter?

12
00:00:44.062 --> 00:00:48.631
Because the program gives every setting
one owning tool, and every server

13
00:00:48.631 --> 00:00:53.050
one patching tool, and both are
recorded in the configuration database.

14
00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:54.775
Arc doesn't change any of that.

15
00:00:54.775 --> 00:00:58.036
What it does is turn the
server into an Azure resource,

16
00:00:58.036 --> 00:01:01.963
and from then on, tags tell Azure's
tools which servers are theirs.

17
00:01:01.963 --> 00:01:05.312
Those tags are written once,
at the moment of connection.

18
00:01:05.312 --> 00:01:09.937
So the onboarding run is the one place
owners get read, checked and carried.

19
00:01:09.937 --> 00:01:15.030
Connect a server without them, and it
belongs to whichever tool reaches it first.

20
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.755
Here's who reads those tags
once a server is connected.

21
00:01:21.755 --> 00:01:24.626
Machine configuration,
from the last chapter,

22
00:01:24.626 --> 00:01:28.705
only enforces on servers whose
configuration owner tag names it.

23
00:01:28.705 --> 00:01:33.742
Update Manager's dynamic scopes can group
machines by tag, and they're evaluated

24
00:01:33.742 --> 00:01:38.168
at every scheduled run, so a scope on
the patch owner tag follows the tag.

25
00:01:38.168 --> 00:01:41.606
The built-in assessment policy
can be scoped by tag too.

26
00:01:41.606 --> 00:01:43.937
And the validation kit
checks afterward for

27
00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:46.205
a server carrying
enforcement it shouldn't.

28
00:01:46.205 --> 00:01:49.718
Every one of them depends on the
tag being right from the start.

29
00:01:49.718 --> 00:01:51.570
Next, which Azure.

30
00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:56.195
Which Azure?

31
00:01:56.195 --> 00:01:57.720
This is easy to get wrong.

32
00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:03.119
The estate is a Microsoft 365 GCC tenant,
and Learn says GCC uses the public

33
00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:08.695
directory, which can't be associated
with an Azure Government subscription.

34
00:02:08.695 --> 00:02:12.120
So the estate's Arc servers go
to a commercial subscription,

35
00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.383
using the agent's default cloud and
the commercial endpoints on the slide.

36
00:02:16.383 --> 00:02:19.254
GCC High and Defense
tenants are different:

37
00:02:19.254 --> 00:02:24.358
they use the government directory, Azure
Government, and the dot U S endpoints.

38
00:02:24.358 --> 00:02:27.610
The code guards both
directions, as we'll see.

39
00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.072
Here's what Microsoft gives you.

40
00:02:33.072 --> 00:02:35.836
Learn documents Arc
onboarding with Ansible

41
00:02:35.836 --> 00:02:38.309
through one role in the Azure collection.

42
00:02:38.309 --> 00:02:41.359
It installs the agent
and registers each host,

43
00:02:41.359 --> 00:02:45.372
and it's idempotent, so a connected
machine is simply skipped.

44
00:02:45.372 --> 00:02:49.857
Learn's page covers the onboarding
role, the variables, and authentication,

45
00:02:49.857 --> 00:02:52.894
recommending the control
node's managed identity

46
00:02:52.894 --> 00:02:55.860
in production because it
removes stored secrets.

47
00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.523
It also suggests scheduling
the job nightly.

48
00:02:58.523 --> 00:03:03.206
But Learn's planning page adds something
the role page doesn't: pilot first,

49
00:03:03.206 --> 00:03:08.020
on non-critical machines, for at least
thirty days, with a rollback plan.

50
00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:16.245
The repository keeps an unmodified copy
of the role, from the released version,

51
00:03:16.245 --> 00:03:20.995
with every file's hash checked against
upstream and against the published package.

52
00:03:20.995 --> 00:03:23.394
The collection is pinned to that version,

53
00:03:23.394 --> 00:03:26.782
and a test fails if the installed
role isn't the pinned copy.

54
00:03:26.782 --> 00:03:30.344
Reading the code turned up two
things Learn's page doesn't say.

55
00:03:30.344 --> 00:03:34.182
The role's default tag is different
from the one Learn's table gives.

56
00:03:34.182 --> 00:03:38.396
And unless you turn it off, it sends
a signal about your subscription,

57
00:03:38.396 --> 00:03:41.632
resource group, tenant and
region back to Microsoft.

58
00:03:41.632 --> 00:03:45.210
The chapter follows the code,
because that's what runs.

59
00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.466
Running the role offline, and reading it,

60
00:03:51.466 --> 00:03:55.110
shows what it leaves to you,
starting with owners and waves.

61
00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:59.123
It connects whatever the inventory
names, with no owner tags.

62
00:03:59.123 --> 00:04:03.798
It has no waves or batches, so one
mistake reaches everything at once.

63
00:04:03.798 --> 00:04:07.356
It can't do a real dry run on
a server without the agent;

64
00:04:07.356 --> 00:04:10.997
it wrongly decides the agent
is installed, then fails.

65
00:04:10.997 --> 00:04:13.906
Passing your own tags
silently drops its default

66
00:04:13.906 --> 00:04:16.885
that keeps database servers
from auto-onboarding.

67
00:04:16.885 --> 00:04:18.935
And it sends that signal by default.

68
00:04:18.935 --> 00:04:22.690
Each gap gets a fix, and
each is pinned by a test.

69
00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.478
The rest of the gaps are about
the cloud and the record.

70
00:04:29.478 --> 00:04:34.208
The role's token is for commercial Resource
Manager, which is right for this estate,

71
00:04:34.208 --> 00:04:37.130
so the role-based playbook
refuses any other cloud,

72
00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:40.052
and a separate playbook
handles Azure Government.

73
00:04:40.052 --> 00:04:43.635
The token is briefly visible
on the server's command line;

74
00:04:43.635 --> 00:04:47.065
the chapter accepts that,
since tokens are short-lived.

75
00:04:47.065 --> 00:04:49.643
Nothing checks the region
against the cloud,

76
00:04:49.643 --> 00:04:53.615
nothing is written down, and Learn's
setup installs whatever's newest.

77
00:04:53.615 --> 00:04:58.900
The verdict: use Microsoft's role,
unchanged, and put the checks around it.

78
00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:05.053
The first change is small but essential:

79
00:05:05.053 --> 00:05:08.621
a patch owner column in the
configuration database export,

80
00:05:08.621 --> 00:05:13.500
right beside the configuration owner, with
the onboarding wave after the region.

81
00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:17.775
The guide had named that field, but
the sample export never carried it.

82
00:05:17.775 --> 00:05:22.791
Look at the server on Amazon's cloud:
Ansible configures it and patches it,

83
00:05:22.791 --> 00:05:27.138
but it still goes to Arc, for
inventory, assessment and Defender.

84
00:05:27.138 --> 00:05:29.163
Its patch owner tag says Ansible,

85
00:05:29.163 --> 00:05:32.463
so Update Manager assesses
it and schedules nothing.

86
00:05:32.463 --> 00:05:38.300
And because this is a GCC estate, the
sample now uses commercial regions.

87
00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:46.858
The inventory script turns each owner
into an Ansible group and an Azure tag,

88
00:05:46.858 --> 00:05:48.842
using the Arc track's spellings;

89
00:05:48.842 --> 00:05:53.650
the validation kit compares them without
caring about case or punctuation.

90
00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:55.450
Three rules fail closed.

91
00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:58.230
An owner name it doesn't
recognize stops the run,

92
00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:02.650
because a typo would quietly drop a
server out of the group that protects it.

93
00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:04.700
A wave that isn't a number stops it.

94
00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:07.637
And a server is only a
candidate if its record is

95
00:06:07.637 --> 00:06:10.650
complete and its tags are
values Arc will accept.

96
00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:14.150
Otherwise it's blocked, with
the reason written down.

97
00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:22.575
Before anything touches a server, the
wave plan runs on the control node alone.

98
00:06:22.575 --> 00:06:27.142
You give it a wave number, and it
writes down exactly what it intends:

99
00:06:27.142 --> 00:06:31.045
every ready server, every
blocked one, the resource group,

100
00:06:31.045 --> 00:06:34.450
region, cloud, both owners
and the exact tag string.

101
00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:39.102
It refuses to go on if there's no wave,
if a region belongs to the other cloud,

102
00:06:39.102 --> 00:06:43.987
if a production server has slipped into
a pilot wave, or if an owner is missing.

103
00:06:43.987 --> 00:06:47.049
Both onboarding playbooks
start with this plan,

104
00:06:47.049 --> 00:06:50.480
so if the plan stops, no
server is contacted at all.

105
00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:55.855
Now the onboarding itself.

106
00:06:55.855 --> 00:07:00.470
The role-based playbook works one wave
at a time: a single canary server,

107
00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:04.767
then five percent, then a quarter,
and it stops at the first failure.

108
00:07:04.767 --> 00:07:07.693
Each server is checked again
before anything happens.

109
00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:11.530
Without an explicit confirmation
flag, it never calls the role;

110
00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:16.180
it just prints what each server would get,
which is the dry run the role can't do.

111
00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:19.877
With confirmation, it calls
Microsoft's role unchanged,

112
00:07:19.877 --> 00:07:24.618
with the database's values and no stored
secret, and writes a result file.

113
00:07:24.618 --> 00:07:28.460
The older step-by-step playbook
stays for Azure Government.

114
00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:33.260
Who's involved?

115
00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:38.394
Platform engineers who run Ansible beside
Arc, the developers who keep the inventory

116
00:07:38.394 --> 00:07:43.022
and playbooks, and the reviewers who read
the plan before and the result after.

117
00:07:43.022 --> 00:07:45.996
The onboarding identity
gets one narrow role:

118
00:07:45.996 --> 00:07:49.673
it can read and create Arc
servers, but it can't delete them.

119
00:07:49.673 --> 00:07:53.251
There's a wrinkle: one Learn page
says it can't manage extensions,

120
00:07:53.251 --> 00:07:57.242
another lists an add-extensions
permission in it, so the chapter takes

121
00:07:57.242 --> 00:08:01.234
the stricter reading and removes
the assignment when the wave closes.

122
00:08:01.234 --> 00:08:04.400
Disconnecting needs a
different, stronger role.

123
00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:12.631
Lab 1 runs on a Linux control node
with no subscription, and after setup

124
00:08:12.631 --> 00:08:17.113
every outbound request goes to a dead
local proxy, so nothing can leave.

125
00:08:17.113 --> 00:08:19.983
Use a disposable node,
because one step runs

126
00:08:19.983 --> 00:08:23.174
Microsoft's role as root
against the node itself.

127
00:08:23.174 --> 00:08:27.007
First, look at what the database
export becomes: four candidates,

128
00:08:27.007 --> 00:08:30.912
and the Amazon-hosted server tagged
with Ansible for both owners.

129
00:08:30.912 --> 00:08:34.675
Then run Microsoft's role,
unchanged, in check mode.

130
00:08:34.675 --> 00:08:39.506
It fails, and the verbose output shows
why: check mode skipped the version check,

131
00:08:39.506 --> 00:08:43.200
so the role concluded the
agent was already installed.

132
00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:48.400
Now the kit's own path.

133
00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:51.094
Plan wave zero, and
it's ready: one server,

134
00:08:51.094 --> 00:08:55.050
with its exact tag string
written to a file you can review.

135
00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:59.547
Dry-run the wave with the role-based
playbook, and it prints what that server would

136
00:08:59.547 --> 00:09:04.112
get, including the restored database default,
while the connect itself is skipped.

137
00:09:04.112 --> 00:09:06.212
Then break the export on purpose.

138
00:09:06.212 --> 00:09:10.037
Put a production server into the
pilot wave, and the plan stops.

139
00:09:10.037 --> 00:09:13.362
Misspell a patch owner,
and the inventory refuses.

140
00:09:13.362 --> 00:09:16.787
Clear one owner, and that server
is blocked with its reason.

141
00:09:16.787 --> 00:09:19.840
Run the suite, and ninety tests pass.

142
00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:26.697
Lab 2 connects one
disposable server for real,

143
00:09:26.697 --> 00:09:31.440
in a commercial test subscription in
the GCC tenant, never production.

144
00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:36.227
The control node runs somewhere with a
managed identity, so no secret is stored.

145
00:09:36.227 --> 00:09:40.663
Prepare a resource group, grant the
onboarding role to that identity

146
00:09:40.663 --> 00:09:44.553
and nothing else, and describe
the one server in a tiny export.

147
00:09:44.553 --> 00:09:49.990
Then the same three commands every
wave uses: plan, dry run, connect.

148
00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:51.993
Check the agent says connected,

149
00:09:51.993 --> 00:09:55.690
the tags match the database,
and the validation kit agrees.

150
00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:59.290
Then back it out in Learn's
order, and file the records.

151
00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:05.814
Each danger has a guard,
and the theme is that

152
00:10:05.814 --> 00:10:09.128
the connect is irreversible
enough to deserve care.

153
00:10:09.128 --> 00:10:13.753
Owners must be present and spelled
right, or the server isn't a candidate.

154
00:10:13.753 --> 00:10:18.252
Patching has one owner, so Update
Manager schedules only what it owns.

155
00:10:18.252 --> 00:10:21.564
Waves, a canary and a pilot
that refuses production

156
00:10:21.564 --> 00:10:24.402
keep one mistake from reaching everything.

157
00:10:24.402 --> 00:10:26.578
The dry run must really be dry.

158
00:10:26.578 --> 00:10:28.515
The cloud must match the tenant.

159
00:10:28.515 --> 00:10:33.377
And defaults that send data or onboard
databases become decisions on record.

160
00:10:33.377 --> 00:10:38.110
Finally, a managed identity means
there's no stored secret to steal.

161
00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:43.210
Here's what done looks like.

162
00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:47.285
Every tier one server in the
export has both owners and a wave,

163
00:10:47.285 --> 00:10:49.322
or a ticket explaining why not.

164
00:10:49.322 --> 00:10:51.035
The cloud matches the tenant.

165
00:10:51.035 --> 00:10:55.885
The wave plan was reviewed and filed before
the run, and the dry run matched it.

166
00:10:55.885 --> 00:10:58.768
The onboarding identity
holds one narrow role,

167
00:10:58.768 --> 00:11:01.872
and the two risky defaults
are decisions on record.

168
00:11:01.872 --> 00:11:05.785
The pilot ran on non-production
servers for at least thirty days.

169
00:11:05.785 --> 00:11:10.723
Afterward, the tags in Azure match the
database and the validation kit is clean.

170
00:11:10.723 --> 00:11:15.736
Next, Chapter 9: drift pipelines and
evidence for the security officer.

171
00:11:15.736 --> 00:11:16.900
Thanks for watching.
